<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/forces-beams-and-bridges">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Structural engineering is <strong>directing forces to the ground through chosen paths</strong>.
    The beam is the core problem — bending creates tension and compression in the same member —
    and bridge types are the span-length answers history has converged on. The foundational
    statics lesson gave you free-body diagrams and ΣF = 0; this page builds the working
    engineer's toolkit on top of them: how to compute the internal forces a beam carries,
    how its cross-section turns moment into stress, why depth beats width by a factor of
    depth, why columns fail sideways long before they crush, and why the bridge type is
    decided before the first member is sized.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="load paths, bending, the two checks, the span ladder" widget="equationStep" config={beamCfg} />

  <h2>Equilibrium: the whole game at small scale</h2>
  <p>
    A structure at rest obeys three equations: forces balance in two directions (ΣF<sub>x</sub> = 0,
    ΣF<sub>y</sub> = 0) and moments balance about any point (ΣM = 0). That is the whole of
    planar statics, and it is enough to find every support reaction of a statically
    determinate structure. Worked example: a 6 m beam on simple supports, carrying a
    distributed load of 5 kN/m (total 30 kN, acting at midspan, 3 m from each support).
    By symmetry each reaction takes half: <strong>15 kN</strong>. Push the load off-centre —
    say a single 10 kN point load 2 m from the left support — and take moments about the
    right support: R<sub>left</sub> × 6 = 10 × 4, so R<sub>left</sub> = 6.67 kN and
    R<sub>right</sub> = 3.33 kN. The closer support carries more; moments about the far
    support is the standard trick that isolates one unknown at a time. The free-body
    diagram discipline (isolate the body, mark every force including the unknowns, solve
    the three equations) is what the previous lessons drilled, and everything in this page
    is downstream of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the <strong>load path</strong>: follow a load from where it enters the structure
    to the soil. A person on an office floor loads the slab, which bends and delivers
    edge loads to the beams, which bend and deliver reactions to columns, which carry the
    load down (in compression) to the foundation, which spreads it onto soil at pressures
    the soil can bear (typically 100–600 kPa for building purposes). Design is making
    every link in that chain exist, continuous, and adequate — the 1994 collapse modes
    are almost always broken or never-imagined load paths (a column removed from under a
    transfer beam; a wall that was load-bearing but not accounted for).
  </p>

  <h2>Shear and moment: what a beam feels inside</h2>
  <p>
    Cut a loaded beam at any station x and imagine holding it together: the two faces
    transmit a <strong>shear force</strong> V (the tendency of the two halves to slide
    vertically) and a <strong>bending moment</strong> M (the tendency to rotate relative
    to each other). Plotting them along the beam gives the shear-force and bending-moment
    diagrams — the two most important drawings in structural engineering, because they say
    where the beam is working hardest. For the uniformly loaded 6 m beam above, shear
    starts at +15 kN at the left support, falls linearly to −15 kN at the right (crossing
    zero at midspan), and the moment grows as a parabola to its maximum exactly where
    shear is zero: M<sub>max</sub> = wL²/8 = 5 × 36 ⁄ 8 = <strong>22.5 kN·m</strong>. Drag
    the load and span below and watch the moment parabola respond — note how doubling the
    span quadruples the peak (L²) while doubling the load only doubles it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the bending-moment diagram of a uniformly loaded beam — M(x) = w·x·(L−x)/2" widget="plotter" config={momentCfg} />

  <h2>From moment to stress: why beams are shaped like I's</h2>
  <p>
    The moment diagram says <em>where</em>; the cross-section says <em>how bad</em>. A beam
    that bends carries compression on the concave face, tension on the convex face, and
    nothing at mid-depth (the neutral axis). Bending stress grows linearly with distance y
    from that axis: <strong>σ = M·y ⁄ I</strong>, where I is the second moment of area —
    the section's area, weighted by the square of its distance from the neutral axis. For
    a rectangle, I = b·h³ ⁄ 12. Read the exponent: depth enters <em>cubed</em>. Double a
    beam's depth (same width, same area, same material) and I multiplies by 8; double its
    width and I merely doubles. The <strong>I-beam</strong> is this fact made into a
    factory product: put the material where the stress is — two flanges at maximum
    distance from the axis — and connect them with a thin web that mainly carries the
    shear. It is the section-shape equivalent of the moment arm idea: distance from the
    axis is leverage over bending.
  </p>

  <h2>The two checks: strength and serviceability</h2>
  <p>
    Worked example with real numbers. A timber floor beam, 6 m span, carrying the 5 kN/m
    above, section 100 mm × 200 mm, E = 10 GPa, allowable bending stress ~35 MPa.
    <strong>Strength check:</strong> I = b·h³/12 = 100 × 200³ ⁄ 12 = 66.7 × 10<sup>6</sup>
    mm⁴; σ = M·y ⁄ I = 22.5 × 10<sup>6</sup> N·mm × 100 mm ⁄ 66.7 × 10<sup>6</sup> mm⁴ ≈
    34 MPa. Within the allowable — the beam will not break. <strong>Deflection check:</strong>
    δ = 5wL⁴ ⁄ (384EI) = 5 × 5 × 6000⁴ ⁄ (384 × 10,000 × 66.7 × 10<sup>6</sup>) ≈
    <strong>127 mm</strong>. The serviceability limit for a floor is typically span/360 ≈
    17 mm. The beam passes strength and fails deflection by a factor of about 8 — it would
    not collapse; it would be a trampoline. This is the standard outcome in preliminary
    design and the reason the widget note says serviceability governs more designs than
    strength: stiffness, not rupture, is usually the binding constraint. The fixes follow
    from the formulas: deflection carries L⁴ (spans are expensive in stiffness) and E·I in
    the denominator (deeper section, stiffer material, or a smaller span). Deepen the same
    beam to 400 mm and I rises 8-fold, bringing deflection to code territory.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "strong enough" means "good enough". A beam can be
  far from failure and unusable — bouncy floors, cracked finishes, rattling glazing.
  Strength (a material property, stress against allowable) and stiffness (a section and
  material property, E·I against limits) are separate checks with separate formulas, and
  mature engineers run the deflection check first because it usually loses.</p>

  <h2>Trusses: geometry doing the work</h2>
  <p>
    A truss replaces the bending beam with a triangulated skeleton. The insight is
    geometric: a triangle of pin-jointed bars cannot change shape without changing a bar's
    length, so a triangulated frame is rigid by construction — and if loads are applied
    only at the joints, every member carries pure axial force (tension or compression),
    no bending anywhere. Each bar then uses its entire cross-section uniformly — the
    efficiency the I-beam only approaches. Analysis is the equilibrium equations applied
    joint by joint (method of joints) or section by section (method of sections), solvable
    by hand, which is why the 19th century — the railway century — built trusses
    everywhere and why they remain the iconic bridge vocabulary. Watch for
    <strong>zero-force members</strong>: truss geometry often keeps members whose only job
    is stability, not load, and a rookie's "optimisation" that removes them can trigger
    collapse under a load case nobody drew.
  </p>

  <h2>Columns: buckling, the sideways failure</h2>
  <p>
    Compression members fail in a different and more surprising way. Euler's analysis
    (1744) gives the critical load at which a slender column bows sideways:
    <strong>P<sub>cr</sub> = π²EI ⁄ L²</strong>. Note what is <em>absent</em>: material
    strength. A column can buckle at loads far below its crushing strength, purely from
    geometry and stiffness. Worked example: a 2 m steel pin-ended strut with I =
    1 × 10<sup>6</sup> mm⁴: P<sub>cr</sub> = π² × 200,000 × 10<sup>6</sup> ⁄ (2000)² ≈
    490 kN. Double its length to 4 m and the capacity falls to a quarter: about 120 kN.
    The inverse-square in L and the linear-in-I (hence cubed-in-depth, again) are the
    whole design story for columns: keep them short, brace them, give them I. Real codes
    wrap Euler's result in slenderness curves (imperfections, end conditions — the
    effective length L<sub>e</sub> substitutes for L), and buckling remains the failure
    mode behind many of history's scaffold and falsework accidents, where a temporary
    structure is all slenderness.
  </p>

  <h2>Bridges: the span decides the type</h2>
  <p>
    Every bridge type is a strategy for spanning distance, and each wins in its regime.
    <strong>Beams</strong> (simple, precast, rolled): the default to ~25–80 m, paying the
    L²-moment and L⁴-deflection taxes. <strong>Trusses</strong> extend the beam by making
    its "flanges" into chords: practical to a few hundred metres. <strong>Arches</strong>
    convert span into compression — stone's one talent — which is why the Roman arch and
    its masonry descendants stand for two millennia: keep the thrust line inside the
    masonry and there is no tension to crack anything. <strong>Suspension bridges</strong>
    (cables in tension between towers, deck hung from them) own the long-span record —
    the Akashi Kaikyō Bridge's 1,991 m main span (1998) — because tension members pay no
    buckling penalty and cable self-weight grows gently with span. <strong>Cable-stayed
    bridges</strong> (fanning straight from tower to deck) dominate the 200–1,000 m range
    on stiffness and economy. The choice is made before member design; the type <em>is</em>
    the design.
  </p>
  <p>
    And when the load is not static, the structure must answer to the wind.
    <strong>Tacoma Narrows, 7 November 1940</strong>: the third-longest suspension span in
    the world, four months old, collapsed in a 67 km/h gale — a wind its strength could
    resist easily. The deck was shallow, stiff in bending, and fatally flexible in twist;
    the wind shed vortices and then coupled with the deck's torsional mode
    (<strong>aeroelastic flutter</strong> — modern analysis, not the "resonance" of the
    classroom myth), and the growing oscillations tore it apart. The only casualty was
    a dog left in the stranded last car — its owner and Professor Farquharson, who had
    gone out to rescue it, escaped the deck themselves. The lesson
    reshaped the profession: structures must be designed for the dynamics of their
    environment, not just the statics of their loads — and bridge decks have been
    aerodynamically profiled ever since (the trapezoid box girder you see everywhere is
    Tacoma's signature, written in cross-section).
  </p>
  <p>
    One more quiet revolution belongs here: <strong>prestressed concrete</strong>. Concrete
    is strong in compression and weak in tension; so before a beam ever carries load,
    high-strength steel tendons inside it are jacked and anchored, squeezing the concrete.
    The service loads now merely relieve some of that pre-compression — net tension never
    appears, cracks never open, and spans that killed reinforced concrete at 20 m run to
    40 m and beyond. Freeway viaducts, parking structures, floor slabs: the 20th
    century's infrastructure is largely this trick, working invisibly inside the grey.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Indeterminacy: more equations than unknowns.</strong> A beam on three supports,
    a frame with rigid joints — these cannot be solved by equilibrium alone because they
    have redundant load paths. That redundancy is not a nuisance but a safety property:
    load finds alternative routes when a member softens or fails. Continuous bridges,
    shear walls, the hyperstatic frames of tall buildings — indeterminacy is engineered
    robustness, bought at the price of solving compatibility of deformations alongside
    equilibrium.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond yield: the other failure modes.</strong> <strong>Fatigue</strong> kills
    members that survive millions of ordinary loads — crack growth per cycle, the reason
    riveted railway bridges and aircraft fuselages have inspection intervals written into
    law (the Comet disasters of 1954 taught aviation this). <strong>Stress
    concentrations</strong> — a hole, a fillet radius too sharp — multiply local stress by
    factors of 2–4 and are where fatigue cracks start; design language is full of them
    (round corners, gradual transitions). <strong>Factor of safety</strong> (typically
    1.5–3 for structures, ~1.1 in aircraft where mass is money) prices our ignorance of
    loads, materials and workmanship; modern limit-state codes split it into partial
    factors on load and material, which is more honest about where the uncertainty lives.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> Structural health monitoring (fibre-optic strain, modal
    sensors) aims to make bridges report their own condition — the field's quiet shift
    from fixed inspection intervals to continuous data. And the coming century's problem
    is the opposite of Tacoma's: an enormous fleet of aging structures built in the
    1960s–70s, needing life assessment under loads and traffic their designers never
    imagined — engineering judgement against entropy, with the mathematics on this page
    as the first instrument.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Petroski, To Engineer Is Human; Gordon, Structures: Or Why Things Don't Fall Down; Hibbeler, Mechanics of Materials; the statics lesson (free-body diagrams, safety factors); the Tacoma Narrows failure investigations (Ammann &amp; von Kármán, 1941).</p>
</TopicPage>
